<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gerald FitzGerald, 14th Earl of Desmond</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1533–<a href="/years/1583">1583</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Gerald FitzGerald, 15th Earl of Desmond, Rebel Earl · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fitzgerald-dynasty">FitzGerald dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gerald FitzGerald, Earl of Desmond, numbered the 14th or 15th earl, was the last of the great FitzGerald lords of Desmond in Munster. Defeated by his rival the Earl of Ormond at Affane in 1565, he spent years in detention in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. He joined the second Desmond Rebellion in 1579, and was hunted down and killed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-kerry">County Kerry</a> in 1583, after which his lands were confiscated.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gerald was the eldest son of James FitzJohn FitzGerald, Earl of Desmond, by his second wife, More O'Carroll, of a native Irish family; a son of the first marriage, Thomas, was considered illegitimate. The FitzGeralds of Desmond were a cadet branch of the Old English Geraldines, and they were at perpetual war with the Butlers, the Earls of Ormond. In 1550, at 17, Gerald married Joan Fitzgerald, then about 40, widow of the 9th Earl of Ormond and mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-butler-10th-earl-of-ormond">Thomas Butler, 10th Earl of Ormond</a>. The childless marriage brought a temporary truce between the families.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He succeeded his father on 14 October 1558 and was knighted at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a> by the Lord Deputy, Thomas Radclyffe, 3rd Earl of Sussex. Summoned to the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a>, he appeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in May 1562, was briefly imprisoned in the Tower of London and was held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> until 1564. Joan died on 2 January 1565, and he soon married Eleanor Butler, daughter of Edmond Butler, 1st Baron Dunboyne.</p><p>On 8 February 1565 the private Battle of Affane was fought on the Blackwater river. Sir Edmund Butler of Cloughgrenan shot Desmond in the hip and about 300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fitzgerald-dynasty">Geraldines</a> were killed. Carried from the field and asked where the great Lord Desmond was now, he answered: &quot;Where but in his proper place, on the necks of the Butlers?&quot; He and his brother Sir John of Desmond were later imprisoned in the Tower and surrendered their lands to the queen in 1568. Released in 1573, he slipped away from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> that November, and on 18 July 1574 the Geraldine chiefs pledged to support him unconditionally; he submitted at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cork">Cork</a> on 2 September.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After his cousin James FitzMaurice FitzGerald landed at Smerwick Bay in 1579, Sir William Pelham proclaimed Desmond a traitor on 1 November. Carrigafoyle Castle and Askeaton Castle fell in April 1580, Sir John was killed in December 1581, and Desmond was attainted in 1582. By June 1583 he was fleeing with four followers. On 11 November 1583 government forces, alerted by a farmer his men had plundered, surprised him in a cabin in Glenageenty Wood near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tralee">Tralee</a> and killed him. His son James became known as the Tower Earl.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gerald FitzGerald, 14th Earl of Desmond” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Gerald FitzGerald</b>, the 15th Earl, rebelled against Elizabeth I in the Desmond Rebellions.</blockquote><a href="/state/earldom-of-desmond">Earldom of Desmond</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.35,-6.26&amp;year=1565" aria-label="Dublin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Gerald FitzGerald, 14th Earl of Desmond"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4681 5552h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4681 5552h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.35,-6.26&amp;year=1565">What was here in 1565? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
